<h2>6. Other Well-Known Metres</h2>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><strong>Indravajrā</strong>: 11 syllables per pāda (त त ज + two guru).</li>
  <li><strong>Vasantatilakā</strong>: 14 syllables per pāda, popular in classical poetry.</li>
  <li><strong>Mandākrāntā</strong>: 17 syllables per pāda, the slow, "gently advancing" metre of Kālidāsa's <em>Meghadūta</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Śārdūlavikrīḍita</strong>: 19 syllables per pāda, used for grand descriptive verses.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Try scanning a verse yourself: split it into syllables, mark each one laghu or guru, then check positions 5, 6 and 7. It is one of the best ways to train your ear for correct Sanskrit recitation.
</p>
